--> E005\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization work covers — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope/approach of productization changes with the cleared model set). E002 carries `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) + content dependency | The validated model list documented is produced by the campaign. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n| (none) E003 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 / Rule 13 config-reference exception | Kueue (opendatahub-io/kueue) and Training Operator (opendatahub-io/training-operator) are different repos with no shared build artifact. The amd.com/gpu quota configuration the training validation consumes is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact — expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E004, never a DAG edge. |\n| (none) E001/E002 ↔ E003/E004 | Rules 14/15 | Training enablement is independent of the serving validation outcome; HLR-3 proceeds regardless of the serving go/no-go. Both run in parallel (Tier 1 work). |\n| (none) E001 → E003/E004 | Availability precondition, not structural | Training validation needs the same AMD Instinct hardware the campaign secures, but that is an availability/coordination risk (noted in Health Warnings), not a build-against dependency. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing functional/performance parity data and the go/no-go scope. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models the campaign clears; gated on E001. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | Split by component boundary (Distributed Workloads / Kueue quota + admission vs. Training Operator / PyTorchJob support), not by priority — both epics are P1 and both are needed for the HLR. Neither is deferrable against the other. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | `bundled`-style note: documentation of all three deliverables is a single docs-authoring work stream (one content effort, one review cycle) — splitting per source epic would fragment the doc topics. Priority is P1 (not P2) via the docs-authoring priority exception: strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1; the P2 label on the HLR reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- Canonical components used verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n  Serving Runtimes (E001, E002), Distributed Workloads (E003), Training\n  Operator (E004), Documentation (E005).\n- **KServe is passive**: the strategy lists it as affected, but no KServe code\n  change is stated or implied — the vLLM ROCm runtime runs under existing\n  KServe InferenceServices, and the same team (RHOAI Serving) owns both.\n  Per the passive-component rule, KServe compatibility is an acceptance\n  criterion on E002 (deploy and serve via KServe InferenceServices without\n  KServe code changes).\n- The strategy's Affected Components section is a bare list (no per-component\n  change or owner detail) and there is no Impacted Teams table. Owner teams\n  were derived from `.context/architecture-context/`: Serving Runtimes and\n  KServe → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n  Distributed Workloads. Documentation has no architecture-context file and no\n  stated owner; E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as owner of the P0\n  deliverable, with Distributed Workloads contributing the training content.\n\n## Open Question Handling\n\n- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: does not change which epics exist or\n  what they do — the campaign exists either way. It is an availability\n  precondition controlled by parties outside the strategy (procurement /\n  partner lab). Handling: securing access is scope on E001; the externally\n  controlled timing is a risk note, not an acceptance criterion. Also flagged\n  in Health Warnings for the training validation epics.\n- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: informational/timing\n  risk — it does not change what gets built, only when releases can align.\n  Handling: risk note on E002 and E004; not an Investigation. Per Rule 21\n  (third party resolves), modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery, no\n  separate upstream epic.\n- **Campaign outcome (go/no-go)**: the outcome selects a subset of the\n  supported model set for productization — a spectrum of scope, not ≤3\n  enumerable outcomes that change epic structure. The productization epic is\n  identical in structure whatever subset clears. Handling: `gated_by` +\n  `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002 rather than conditional branch\n  files (branch epics would duplicate one surface with only the model list\n  differing).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections** (health warnings; decomposition proceeded with\n  available information): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n  Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, no Non-Functional\n  Requirements, no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Per-epic\n  acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and the problem statement.\n- **Availability precondition — AMD Instinct hardware**: procurement or\n  partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. It gates the\n  *execution* of E001's campaign and the validation portions of E004 (and\n  E003's on-hardware admission validation), but it is not an acceptance\n  criterion on any epic; it is recorded as a risk on those epics. If access\n  slips, all hardware-dependent validation