{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-17ec148e-7261-458d-8923-4c7ca5fe16ea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86c11dfe0703900b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-545mdu04/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct vLLM serving validation campaign (functional/performance parity, go/no-go)\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic resolves the central unknown of the AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement\nstrategy: whether vLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators achieves\nfunctional and performance parity with the platform's CUDA-based vLLM runtime\nfor the supported model set. It runs a validation campaign on physical AMD\nInstinct hardware, produces first-hand functional and performance parity data\nper model, and delivers the go/no-go recommendation that bounds the scope of\nthe vLLM ROCm serving-runtime productization work.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct GPU nodes (procurement or partner-lab\n  access) — the epic's work includes securing access; the timing of external\n  granting is a risk, not a deliverable condition.\n- Define the validation campaign methodology: the supported model set under\n  test, functional pass/fail criteria per model, and performance parity\n  measurement methodology against the CUDA baseline.\n- Execute the campaign: run vLLM serving of the supported model set on ROCm on\n  the AMD Instinct nodes, collecting functional results and performance\n  metrics per model.\n- Produce the validation report: per-model functional pass/fail, performance\n  parity data, and the go/no-go recommendation with the list of models cleared\n  for productization.\n- Record driver and ROCm runtime versions used, so the productization and\n  documentation work can reference validated configurations.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The validation campaign plan is documented and covers every model in the\n  supported model set, with a defined functional pass/fail criterion and a\n  defined performance parity measurement for each.\n- Functional results exist for every model in the supported model set: each\n  model is marked pass or fail against its criterion.\n- Performance parity data is captured for every functionally passing model,\n  measured against the CUDA vLLM baseline under the documented methodology.\n- The validation report is published and states, per model: functional result,\n  performance parity data, and driver/ROCm runtime versions used.\n- The report delivers a go/no-go recommendation enumerating the models cleared\n  for productization (the list may be empty, partial, or complete — the\n  deliverable is the decision data, not a particular outcome).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware\n  establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n  supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Hardware access is an availability precondition controlled by parties\n  outside the strategy (procurement or partner-lab negotiation); its timing\n  cannot be compressed by engineering effort and gates campaign execution.\n- Upstream ROCm runtime image cadence is controlled by AMD and upstream\n  communities; the campaign should pin and record the versions it validates.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":34351,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96bb78b90d3832b2","agent_id":"a183521e810571ad7","session_id":"1dade12c-3f35-491e-b719-6185ca00038a","uuid":"1fde17d0-7b6a-4213-940c-faa5cacf1937","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:56:20.495Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020 into epics"}
