{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-909aec1947086c3a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans four components, two owning teams, and\n15\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams (hardware validation campaign,\n16\tserving-runtime productization, training-stack enablement, documentation), and\n17\tcontains genuine unknowns (hardware access, campaign go/no-go outcome) that\n18\tchange downstream scope. Not docs-only: three components require code/config\n19\twork.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct vLLM serving validation campaign (functional/performance parity, go/no-go) | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling and admission for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorchJob ROCm training support (single- and multi-GPU) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD hardware support, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n30\t\n31\t## Dependency DAG\n32\t\n33\t```mermaid\n34\tgraph TD\n35\t    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n36\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n37\t    E002 --> E005\n38\t    E003[\"E003 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E005\n39\t    E004[\"E004 PyTorchJob ROCm training\"] --> E005\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n43\t\n44\t## DAG Justification\n45\t\n46\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n47\t|------|------|-----------|\n48\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization work covers — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope/approach of productization changes with the cleared model set). E002 carries `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n49\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) + content dependency | The validated model list documented is produced by the campaign. |\n50\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n51\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n52\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n53\t| (none) E003 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 / Rule 13 config-reference exception | Kueue (opendatahub-io/kueue) and Training Operator (opendatahub-io/training-operator) are different repos with no shared build artifact. The amd.com/gpu quota configuration the training validation consumes is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact — expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E004, never a DAG edge. |\n54\t| (none) E001/E002 ↔ E003/E004 | Rules 14/15 | Training enablement is independent of the serving validation outcome; HLR-3 proceeds regardless of the serving go/no-go. Both run in parallel (Tier 1 work). |\n55\t| (none) E001 → E003/E004 | Availability precondition, not structural | Training validation needs the same AMD Instinct hardware the campaign secures, but that is an availability/coordination risk (noted in Health Warnings), not a build-against dependency. |\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n58\t\n59\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n60\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n61\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing functional/performance parity data and the go/no-go scope. |\n62\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models the campaign clears; gated on E001. |\n63\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | Split by component boundary (Distributed Workloads / Kueue quota + admission vs. Training Operator / PyTorchJob support), not by priority — both epics are P1 and both are needed for the HLR. Neither is deferrable against the other. |\n64\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | `bundled`-style note: documentation of all three deliverables is a single docs-authoring work stream (one content effort, one review cycle) — splitting per source epic would fragment the doc topics. Priority is P1 (not P2) via the docs-authoring priority exception: strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1; the P2 label on the HLR reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n65\t\n66\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n67\t\n68\t## Component Mapping Notes\n69\t\n70\t- Canonical components used verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n71\t  Serving Runtimes (E001, E002), Distributed Workloads (E003), Training\n72\t  Operator (E004), Documentation (E005).\n73\t- **KServe is passive**: the strategy lists it as affected, but no KServe code\n74\t  change is stated or implied — the vLLM ROCm runtime runs under existing\n75\t  KServe InferenceServices, and the same team (RHOAI Serving) owns both.\n76\t  Per the passive-component rule, KServe compatibility is an acceptance\n77\t  criterion on E002 (deploy and serve via KServe InferenceServices without\n78\t  KServe code changes).\n79\t- The strategy's Affected Components section is a bare list (no per-component\n80\t  change or owner detail) and there is no Impacted Teams table. Owner teams\n81\t  were derived from `.context/architecture-context/`: Serving Runtimes and\n82\t  KServe → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n83\t  Distributed Workloads. Documentation has no architecture-context file and no\n84\t  stated owner; E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as owner of the P0\n85\t  deliverable, with Distributed Workloads contributing the training content.\n86\t\n87\t## Open Question Handling\n88\t\n89\t- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: does not change which epics exist or\n90\t  what they do — the campaign exists either way. It is an availability\n91\t  precondition controlled by parties outside the strategy (procurement /\n92\t  partner lab). Handling: securing access is scope on E001; the externally\n93\t  controlled timing is a risk note, not an acceptance criterion. Also flagged\n94\t  in Health Warnings for the training validation epics.\n95\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: informational/timing\n96\t  risk — it does not change what gets built, only when releases can align.\n97\t  Handling: risk note on E002 and E004; not an Investigation. Per Rule 21\n98\t  (third party resolves), modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery, no\n99\t  separate upstream epic.\n100\t- **Campaign outcome (go/no-go)**: the outcome selects a subset of the\n101\t  supported model set for productization — a spectrum of scope, not ≤3\n102\t  enumerable outcomes that change epic structure. The productization epic is\n103\t  identical in structure whatever subset clears. Handling: `gated_by` +\n104\t  `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002 rather than conditional branch\n105\t  files (branch epics would duplicate one surface with only the model list\n106\t  differing).\n107\t\n108\t## Health Warnings\n109\t\n110\t- **Missing strategy sections** (health warnings; decomposition proceeded with\n111\t  available information): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n112\t  Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, no Non-Functional\n113\t  Requirements, no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Per-epic\n114\t  acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and the problem statement.\n115\t- **Availability precondition — AMD Instinct hardware**: procurement or\n116\t  partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. It gates the\n117\t  *execution* of E001's campaign and the validation portions of E004 (and\n118\t  E003's on-hardware admission validation), but it is not an acceptance\n119\t  criterion on any epic; it is recorded as a risk on those epics. If access\n120\t  slips, all hardware-dependent validation slips with it — the single largest\n121\t  schedule risk in this strategy.\n122\t- **Timing risk — upstream ROCm cadence**: AMD/upstream ROCm runtime image\n123\t  releases may not align with platform release trains; affects E002 (which\n124\t  ROCm image baseline to productize) and E004 (PyTorch ROCm training images).\n125\t- **Scope trap watch**: HLR-2 says \"for the models the validation campaign\n126\t  clears\" — the productization epic's scope is intentionally open until the\n127\t  campaign reports. Reviewers should not read E002's scope as unbounded; it is\n128\t  bounded by the campaign's cleared-model list.\n129\t- No priority inversions detected: P0 work (campaign, productization) is\n130\t  gated serially; P1 training work runs in parallel; docs priority follows the\n131\t  docs-authoring exception (Major → P1).\n132\t\n133\t## Tiered Delivery\n134\t\n135\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, starts immediately)**: E001 (validation\n136\t  campaign — risk-reduction deliverable: parity data + go/no-go scope, with\n137\t  value even if productization defers) and E003/E004 (training enablement on\n138\t  ROCm, independent of the serving go/no-go).\n139\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 (serving runtime productization) — gated by the\n140\t  campaign outcome and, in timing, by upstream ROCm image cadence. E005\n141\t  (documentation) is last per Rule 11 and completes both tiers.\n142\t\n143\t## Re-split Triggers\n144\t\n145\t- E001 stays one epic (a single campaign over one model set) even if it\n146\t  exceeds a sprint of effort: splitting the campaign would fragment the\n147\t  parity-comparison methodology. Re-split trigger: if the supported model set\n148\t  is large enough that the campaign exceeds ~2 sprints, split by model family\n149\t  into per-family campaign epics sharing the methodology.\n150\t- E002 stays one epic (single mechanical productization pass over the vLLM\n151\t  runtime surface). Re-split trigger: if the cleared model list requires more\n152\t  than one distinct runtime variant (e.g., different ROCm baselines per model\n153\t  family), split into per-variant productization epics.\n154\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b47f6c2c089dde6","agent_id":"a057933bc08afe678","session_id":"1dade12c-3f35-491e-b719-6185ca00038a","uuid":"3869d8b5-de1d-4b21-bf2f-82d6c79f8c16","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:58:26.424Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-909aec1947086c3a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
