consumes it as a configuration reference;\n67\tE001 produces the validated model list while E005 documents it — neither pair\n68\trebuilds the other's surface. No work-product mixing: the \"document validated\n69\tbaselines\" bullets in E001/E004 are engineering validation records, while all\n70\tcustomer-facing content authoring lives in E005. E001's potential overage is a\n71\tmanaged watch item, not a defect — the summary's Re-split Triggers section\n72\trecords a concrete trigger (split by model family if the campaign exceeds ~2\n73\tsprints), so no split suggestion is added here.\n74\t\n75\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n76\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its go/no-go outcome changes E002's scope, and\n77\tE002 correctly carries `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` with both\n78\t`gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and the\n79\t`gated_by` target appears in E002's direct dependencies. However, E005 lists the\n80\tInvestigation epic RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 in its `dependencies` while\n81\t`gated_by` is null. The E001→E005 edge is not a Rule 11 edge (E001 is an\n82\tInvestigation, not an Implementation); it exists because E005's documented\n83\tvalidated model list — including which models are documented as unsupported on\n84\tROCm — is exactly E001's output, so by definition the Investigation outcome\n85\tchanges E005's content scope. An Investigation dependency without `gated_by` is\n86\ta major issue: fix by setting `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` with a\n87\t`gate_failure_impact` (e.g. action `rewrite`: the documented model list rewrites\n88\twith the campaign outcome; the epic exists either way), or by removing the\n89\tdirect edge if the model-list content is consumed transitively via E002 — though\n90\tnote the direct edge is the defensible shape, since E002 may not be undertaken\n91\tat all when nothing clears, while E005 still must document the failed models.\n92\tMajor issue forces this criterion to 0 per the scoring rules.\n93\t\n94\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n95\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001 (Investigation) carries\n96\t`investigation_signals`; E002–E005 (Implementation) carry `ai_signals`; no\n97\tcrossover. All five ai-signals rationale files exist and their table values\n98\tmatch the frontmatter values exactly. Calibration verified against `.context`:\n99\tE002's `architecture_claims: 1` is corroborated by\n100\t`architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/serving-runtimes.md` (\"only CUDA\n101\t(NVIDIA) builds are currently productized — ROCm and Gaudi builds exist upstream\n102\tbut are not [shipped]\"); E004's by `training-operator.md` (NVIDIA GPU Operator\n103\tassumption); E003's by `distributed-workloads.md` (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue,\n104\twaitForPodsReady). E005's `architecture_claims: 0` is justified — no\n105\tarchitecture-context file covers Documentation and the epic's scope makes no\n106\tarchitecture claim. E001's investigation signals are well calibrated\n107\t(`source_accessibility: 0` because the parity data does not exist in any\n108\trepository and must be generated on hardware; `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2`\n109\tmatching the strategy's statement that no AMD hardware exists in the CI fleet).\n110\tNo arithmetic or threshold checks performed, per instructions.\n111\t\n112\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n113\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs: E005\n114\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC (\"reviewed by the owning engineers\n115\tof the serving runtime and training stack\"); no konflux-chain or\n116\treplacement/modification epics exist (the ROCm runtime is additive — the CUDA\n117\tpath is untouched and selectable via ServingRuntime, so no rollback/feature-flag\n118\tAC is required). Soft-AC scan is clean: the externally controlled hardware\n119\tavailability is correctly kept in Risks/Notes on E001/E003/E004 and in the\n120\tsummary's Health Warnings, not filed as an AC; E001's ACs all state conditions\n121\ton the epic's own deliverables (the report and its per-model results). E004's\n122\t\"quota configuration is available as a configuration reference at validation\n123\ttime\" is a condition on a sibling in-strategy epic's deliverable (E003) framed\n124\tas the Rule 13 config-reference AC gate — acceptable, not a soft AC. No epic\n125\tbody references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan; references appear\n126\tonly in frontmatter where they are structural).\n127\t\n128\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n129\tAll strategy scope is covered: the validation campaign (E001), serving-runtime\n130\tproductization (E002), Kueue quota/admission and PyTorchJob training halves of\n131\tHLR-3 (E003, E004), and documentation (E005). The out-of-scope boundaries\n132\t(non-AMD accelerators, GPU sharing/partitioning) are carried forward and\n133\tdocumented as unsupported in E005 rather than silently dropped. KServe is\n134\tcorrectly handled as a passive component with a compatibility AC on E002. Both\n135\tstrategy open questions are explicitly dispositioned (hardware access as an\n136\texternal availability precondition/risk; upstream ROCm cadence as a timing risk\n137\tper Rule 21) rather than dropped, and the campaign outcome is handled as a gate.\n138\tStrategy-level risks are carried into epic Risks/Notes and the summary's Health\n139\tWarnings. The docs-authoring epic required by HLR-4 is present and blocked last\n140\tper Rule 11.\n141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9771db83a6f0ef4d","agent_id":"a9fb23f5099235ee4","session_id":"1dade12c-3f35-491e-b719-6185ca00038a","uuid":"03b10fce-554e-4486-bb02-dac4bc5f5e9e","timestamp":"2026-10-23T20:01:34.749Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-974c786026567866","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
