{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8778bf46966afd61","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training)\n10\t\n11\t## Triage\n12\t\n13\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans four components, two owning teams, and\n14\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams (hardware validation campaign,\n15\tserving-runtime productization, training-stack enablement, documentation), and\n16\tcontains genuine unknowns (hardware access, campaign go/no-go outcome) that\n17\tchange downstream scope. Not docs-only: three components require code/config\n18\twork.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct vLLM serving validation campaign (functional/performance parity, go/no-go) | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling and admission for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorchJob ROCm training support (single- and multi-GPU) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD hardware support, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n35\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n36\t    E002 --> E005\n37\t    E003[\"E003 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E005\n38\t    E004[\"E004 PyTorchJob ROCm training\"] --> E005\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n42\t\n43\t## DAG Justification\n44\t\n45\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n46\t|------|------|-----------|\n47\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization work covers — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope/approach of productization changes with the cleared model set). E002 carries `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n48\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) + content dependency | The validated model list documented is produced by the campaign. |\n49\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n50\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n51\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n52\t| (none) E003 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 / Rule 13 config-reference exception | Kueue (opendatahub-io/kueue) and Training Operator (opendatahub-io/training-operator) are different repos with no shared build artifact. The amd.com/gpu quota configuration the training validation consumes is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact — expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E004, never a DAG edge. |\n53\t| (none) E001/E002 ↔ E003/E004 | Rules 14/15 | Training enablement is independent of the serving validation outcome; HLR-3 proceeds regardless of the serving go/no-go. Both run in parallel (Tier 1 work). |\n54\t| (none) E001 → E003/E004 | Availability precondition, not structural | Training validation needs the same AMD Instinct hardware the campaign secures, but that is an availability/coordination risk (noted in Health Warnings), not a build-against dependency. |\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n59\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n60\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing functional/performance parity data and the go/no-go scope. |\n61\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models the campaign clears; gated on E001. |\n62\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | Split by component boundary (Distributed Workloads / Kueue quota + admission vs. Training Operator / PyTorchJob support), not by priority — both epics are P1 and both are needed for the HLR. Neither is deferrable against the other. |\n63\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | `bundled`-style note: documentation of all three deliverables is a single docs-authoring work stream (one content effort, one review cycle) — splitting per source epic would fragment the doc topics. Priority is P1 (not P2) via the docs-authoring priority exception: strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1; the P2 label on the HLR reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n64\t\n65\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n66\t\n67\t## Component Mapping Notes\n68\t\n69\t- Canonical components used verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n70\t  Serving Runtimes (E001, E002), Distributed Workloads (E003), Training\n71\t  Operator (E004), Documentation (E005).\n72\t- **KServe is passive**: the strategy lists it as affected, but no KServe code\n73\t  change is stated or implied — the vLLM ROCm runtime runs under existing\n74\t  KServe InferenceServices, and the same team (RHOAI Serving) owns both.\n75\t  Per the passive-component rule, KServe compatibility is an acceptance\n76\t  criterion on E002 (deploy and serve via KServe InferenceServices without\n77\t  KServe code changes).\n78\t- The strategy's Affected Components section is a bare list (no per-component\n79\t  change or owner detail) and there is no Impacted Teams table. Owner teams\n80\t  were derived from `.context/architecture-context/`: Serving Runtimes and\n81\t  KServe → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n82\t  Distributed Workloads. Documentation has no architecture-context file and no\n83\t  stated owner; E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as owner of the P0\n84\t  deliverable, with Distributed Workloads contributing the training content.\n85\t\n86\t## Open Question Handling\n87\t\n88\t- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: does not change which epics exist or\n89\t  what they do — the campaign exists either way. It is an availability\n90\t  precondition controlled by parties outside the strategy (procurement /\n91\t  partner lab). Handling: securing access is scope on E001; the externally\n92\t  controlled timing is a risk note, not an acceptance criterion. Also flagged\n93\t  in Health Warnings for the training validation epics.\n94\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: informational/timing\n95\t  risk — it does not change what gets built, only when releases can align.\n96\t  Handling: risk note on E002 and E004; not an Investigation. Per Rule 21\n97\t  (third party resolves), modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery, no\n98\t  separate upstream epic.\n99\t- **Campaign outcome (go/no-go)**: the outcome selects a subset of the\n100\t  supported model set for productization — a spectrum of scope, not ≤3\n101\t  enumerable outcomes that change epic structure. The productization epic is\n102\t  identical in structure whatever subset clears. Handling: `gated_by` +\n103\t  `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002 rather than conditional branch\n104\t  files (branch epics would duplicate one surface with only the model list\n105\t  differing).\n106\t\n107\t## Health Warnings\n108\t\n109\t- **Missing strategy sections** (health warnings; decomposition proceeded with\n110\t  available information): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n111\t  Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, no Non-Functional\n112\t  Requirements, no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Per-epic\n113\t  acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and the problem statement.\n114\t- **Availability precondition — AMD Instinct hardware**: procurement or\n115\t  partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. It gates the\n116\t  *execution* of E001's campaign and the validation portions of E004 (and\n117\t  E003's on-hardware admission validation), but it is not an acceptance\n118\t  criterion on any epic; it is recorded as a risk on those epics. If access\n119\t  slips, all hardware-dependent validation slips with it — the single largest\n120\t  schedule risk in this strategy.\n121\t- **Timing risk — upstream ROCm cadence**: AMD/upstream ROCm runtime image\n122\t  releases may not align with platform release trains; affects E002 (which\n123\t  ROCm image baseline to productize) and E004 (PyTorch ROCm training images).\n124\t- **Scope trap watch**: HLR-2 says \"for the models the validation campaign\n125\t  clears\" — the productization epic's scope is intentionally open until the\n126\t  campaign reports. Reviewers should not read E002's scope as unbounded; it is\n127\t  bounded by the campaign's cleared-model list.\n128\t- No priority inversions detected: P0 work (campaign, productization) is\n129\t  gated serially; P1 training work runs in parallel; docs priority follows the\n130\t  docs-authoring exception (Major → P1).\n131\t\n132\t## Tiered Delivery\n133\t\n134\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, starts immediately)**: E001 (validation\n135\t  campaign — risk-reduction deliverable: parity data + go/no-go scope, with\n136\t  value even if productization defers) and E003/E004 (training enablement on\n137\t  ROCm, independent of the serving go/no-go).\n138\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 (serving runtime productization) — gated by the\n139\t  campaign outcome and, in timing, by upstream ROCm image cadence. E005\n140\t  (documentation) is last per Rule 11 and completes both tiers.\n141\t\n142\t## Re-split Triggers\n143\t\n144\t- E001 stays one epic (a single campaign over one model set) even if it\n145\t  exceeds a sprint of effort: splitting the campaign would fragment the\n146\t  parity-comparison methodology. Re-split trigger: if the supported model set\n147\t  is large enough that the campaign exceeds ~2 sprints, split by model family\n148\t  into per-family campaign epics sharing the methodology.\n149\t- E002 stays one epic (single mechanical productization pass over the vLLM\n150\t  runtime surface). Re-split trigger: if the cleared model list requires more\n151\t  than one distinct runtime variant (e.g., different ROCm baselines per model\n152\t  family), split into per-variant productization epics.\n153\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9771db83a6f0ef4d","agent_id":"a9fb23f5099235ee4","session_id":"1dade12c-3f35-491e-b719-6185ca00038a","uuid":"51a10244-4693-428d-b2bd-84e6c17517d5","timestamp":"2026-10-23T20:01:42.996Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8778bf46966afd61","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
